  <span class="tag">Special feature</span>
  <h1>Quantum &amp; the Upanishads: what the founders actually read</h1>

  <section class="explain-simple">
    <div class="kicker">In simple words</div>
    <p>
      You've probably heard the story: when the founders of quantum physics couldn't make sense of their own
      discoveries, they turned to the <strong>Upanishads and the Vedas</strong> — and found the answers there.
      The truth is more interesting, and more honest. <strong>Three of the giants genuinely engaged with Hindu
      thought</strong> — Schrödinger deeply, Oppenheimer seriously, Heisenberg briefly — but the Upanishads
      never supplied a single equation. What they supplied was a <em>vocabulary</em>: a way to think and talk
      about a reality stranger than classical physics allowed.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Schrödinger</strong> read the Upanishads from 1918 (through Schopenhauer) and built his
      philosophy of mind on the Chandogya Upanishad's <em>Tat tvam asi</em> — "that thou art." <strong>Heisenberg</strong>
      spent one afternoon with <strong>Rabindranath Tagore</strong> in Calcutta in 1929 — four years <em>after</em>
      inventing quantum mechanics — and said it later helped him accept ideas that had "seemed so crazy."
      <strong>Oppenheimer</strong> learned Sanskrit from 1933 to read the <strong>Bhagavad Gita</strong> in the
      original, and at the Trinity test its most terrifying verse was in his mind. <strong>Bohr's</strong> famous
      yin-yang coat of arms? That's <strong>Taoist</strong>, not Hindu — and he never engaged with Hindu thought at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page tells the documented history — with sources — and separates it from the myths the internet
      keeps repeating. The real story doesn't need exaggeration: it is remarkable enough.
    </p>
  </section>

  <details class="deeper">
    <summary>Go deeper — the four founders, the scriptures, and the myth-busting</summary>
    <div class="deeper-body">
      <h3>Schrödinger &amp; Vedanta — the deepest documented engagement</h3>
      <p>
        Of all the quantum founders, <strong>Erwin Schrödinger</strong> (1887–1961) engaged most seriously with
        Indian philosophy — and the scholarship on it is now precise. His notebooks from around <strong>1918</strong>,
        near the end of WWI, already show a preoccupation with Indian thought; by his own recollection he met the
        "doctrine of unity" <strong>through Arthur Schopenhauer</strong> — who had read Anquetil-Duperron's 1801 Latin
        translation of the Upanishads (itself based on Prince Dara Shukoh's 1657 Persian translation, carrying
        commentary traceable to the 8th-century philosopher <strong>Shankara</strong>). Archival research (Latten,
        2025, peer-reviewed) shows it was specifically <strong>Shankara's Advaita Vedanta</strong> reading that
        Schrödinger absorbed — not "the Upanishads" in some unmediated form.
      </p>
      <p>
        In philosophical writings dated <strong>1925</strong> — a year <em>before</em> wave mechanics — he formulated
        his <strong>"doctrine of identity"</strong>: the radical unity of all individuals and of the self with the
        world. The famous passage: <em>"This life of yours which you are living is not merely a piece of this entire
        existence, but in a certain sense the whole... This, as we know, is what the Brahmins express in that sacred,
        mystic formula which is yet really so simple and so clear: <strong>Tat tvam asi, this is you.</strong>"</em>
        He called this "the profound rightness of the basic conviction of Vedanta."
      </p>
      <p>
        His own words, across decades: <em>"The multiplicity is only apparent. This is the doctrine of the
        Upanishads."</em> (<em>What is Life?</em>, 1944, p. 129). <em>"There is obviously only one alternative,
        namely the unification of minds or consciousnesses... in truth there is only one mind. This is the doctrine
        of the Upanishads."</em> (<em>Mind and Matter</em>, 1958). And the much-quoted prescription: <em>"I do
        believe that this is precisely the point where our present way of thinking does need to be amended, perhaps
        by a bit of blood-transfusion from Eastern thought... We do not wish to lose the logical precision that our
        scientific thought has reached, and that is unparalleled anywhere at any epoch."</em> His <em>My View of the
        World</em> (1961) culminates in a chapter called <strong>"The Vedantic Vision"</strong>: <em>"From the early
        great Upanishads the recognition ATMAN = BRAHMAN was in Indian thought considered, far from being
        blasphemous, to represent the quintessence of deepest insight into the happenings of the world."</em>
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Did Vedanta shape the 1926 wave mechanics?</strong> The honest scholarly answer: the philosophy
        predated the physics, but <strong>no one has shown it produced the physics</strong>. The mathematics came
        from Hamilton–Jacobi theory, de Broglie's matter waves, and eigenvalue problems — standard physics lineage.
        What scholars defend is subtler: Michel Bitbol concluded the Vedantic-like view served as "a broad framework,
        and as a subliminal inspiration, in all parts of his work, including in theoretical physics" — subliminal,
        not textual. <strong>Verdict: verified</strong> as a deep, lifelong philosophical engagement; <strong>myth</strong>
        that he derived the wave equation from the Vedas. (Claims that he "became a Hindu," kept scriptures at his
        bedside, or named his dog Atman are unverified anecdotes.)
      </p>

      <h3>Heisenberg &amp; Tagore — one afternoon, decades of retelling</h3>
      <p>
        On <strong>4 October 1929</strong>, during a world lecture tour, <strong>Werner Heisenberg</strong> visited
        the University of Calcutta and that afternoon met <strong>Rabindranath Tagore</strong> (brought by the physicist
        D. M. Bose, nephew of J. C. Bose). The next day Heisenberg wrote his parents: <em>"Nachmittags war ich bei
        dem indischen Dichter Rabindranath Tagore zu Gast…"</em> — "In the afternoon I was the guest of the Indian
        poet Rabindranath Tagore."
      </p>
      <p>
        Heisenberg's own account survives only through <strong>Fritjof Capra</strong>, who met him in Munich in 1972
        and recorded it in <em>Uncommon Wisdom</em> (1988, p. 43): <em>"'After these conversations with Tagore,' he
        said, 'some of the ideas that had seemed so crazy suddenly made much more sense. That was a great help for
        me.'"</em> Capra's paraphrase: Heisenberg "began to see that the recognition of relativity, interconnectedness,
        and impermanence as fundamental aspects of physical reality, which had been so difficult for himself and his
        fellow physicists, was the very basis of the Indian spiritual traditions." In a 1971 letter to Capra, Heisenberg
        wrote: <em>"Die Verwandtschaft der alten östlichen Lehren mit den philosophischen Konsequenzen der modernen
        Quantentheorie haben mich immer wieder fasziniert"</em> — "The kinship between the ancient Eastern teachings
        and the philosophical consequences of modern quantum theory have fascinated me again and again."
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Correctives Capra himself later made:</strong> his schoolboy German first misled him — <em>"bei Tagore
        zu Gast"</em> meant a visit, not a house-guest stay, and <em>"wir sprachen lange"</em> meant <strong>one long
        afternoon conversation</strong>, not "several long conversations." And there is a counter-voice: Heisenberg's
        widow Elisabeth told Tagore's biographers (1990) that her husband <em>"was not too much impressed"</em> at the time.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Chronology kills the strong claim:</strong> matrix mechanics — June <strong>1925</strong>. Uncertainty
        principle — <strong>1927</strong>. Tagore meeting — October <strong>1929</strong>, four years after the
        breakthrough. Indian philosophy did not and could not have led Heisenberg to his discoveries; at most, by his
        own 1972 recollection, it later helped him <em>accept</em> them. (The widely circulated quote "Quantum theory
        will not look ridiculous to people who have read Vedanta" has <strong>no traceable primary source</strong> —
        treat it as apocryphal.)
      </p>

      <h3>Oppenheimer &amp; the Bhagavad Gita — the best-documented case</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>J. Robert Oppenheimer</strong> began serious Sanskrit study <strong>in 1933 with Arthur W. Ryder at
        Berkeley</strong>, specifically to read the Bhagavad Gita in the original. He wrote "gushingly" about it to his
        brother Frank, and quoted from the Gita at the Los Alamos memorial service for President Roosevelt in April 1945.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Trinity test: <strong>16 July 1945</strong>. The famous line — <em>"Now I am become Death, the destroyer of
        worlds"</em> — is attested <strong>only</strong> in a <strong>1965 NBC television interview</strong>, twenty years
        after the fact: <em>"We knew the world would not be the same. A few people laughed, a few people cried. Most
        people were silent. I remembered the line from the Hindu scripture, the Bhagavad-Gita; Vishnu is trying to
        persuade the Prince that he should do his duty and, to impress him, he takes on his multi-armed form and says,
        'Now I am become Death, the destroyer of worlds.' I suppose we all thought that, one way or another."</em>
        Caveats historians stress: his brother Frank, who was present, recalled Oppenheimer saying only <strong>"it
        worked"</strong>; the Gita quote didn't circulate as Los Alamos lore until the late 1940s–50s; and Oppenheimer
        says "Vishnu" — the speaker in the text is <strong>Krishna</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The verse is <strong>Bhagavad Gita 11.32</strong>, Krishna in his terrifying cosmic form (Vishvarupa):
        <em>kālo 'smi loka-kṣaya-kṛt pravṛddho</em> — <em>"Time I am, destroyer of the worlds."</em> <em>Kāla</em>
        primarily means <strong>time</strong>; Ryder rendered it "Death" — idiosyncratic but defensible, since in context
        the expanse of time is a deadly one. Historian James Hijiya's crucial corrective: Oppenheimer is not casting
        himself as Krishna/Death — he is <strong>Arjuna</strong>, the reluctant warrior enjoined to do a terrible duty
        by something larger than himself. The Gita's teaching is <strong>detachment in action</strong> (cf. 2.47, the
        "fruits of action") — a vocabulary of duty-without-clinging that helps explain how the man who called the bomb
        a "sin" never said he regretted building it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Oppenheimer kept using the Gita as moral vocabulary: "fruits of work" in postwar lectures on scientists'
        responsibility, and in his 1953 Reith Lectures: <em>"Even in our own culture, they have a history, and in
        Buddhist and Hindu thought a more considerable and central place. What we shall find is an exemplification,
        an encouragement, and a refinement of old wisdom."</em> In 1963 he named the Gita among the ten books that most
        shaped his philosophy of life. (<strong>Debunked along the way:</strong> "Access to the Vedas is the greatest
        privilege of this century" is <strong>Schopenhauer's</strong> line, not Oppenheimer's.)
      </p>

      <h3>Bohr — the Taoist clarification</h3>
      <p>
        The famous fact is real but routinely mislabeled. When <strong>Niels Bohr</strong> received the Order of the
        Elephant in <strong>1947</strong>, he designed his own coat of arms: a <strong>taijitu (yin-yang symbol)</strong>
        with the Latin motto <em>Contraria sunt complementa</em> — "opposites are complementary." The taijitu is a
        <strong>Chinese Taoist</strong> symbol; Bohr chose it to emblemize his <strong>complementarity principle</strong>
        (wave and particle descriptions as mutually exclusive yet jointly necessary).
      </p>
      <p>
        On Indian/Hindu thought specifically: <strong>no documented engagement exists</strong>. Bohr visited India in
        1960, but the documented purpose was scientific cooperation with Homi J. Bhabha around India's nuclear program.
        Claims of Bohr reading the Upanishads — including "I go into the Upanishads to ask questions" — are
        <strong>unsourced or misattribute Schrödinger's words to him</strong>. A 2026 meme about Bohr meeting the holy
        woman Anandamayi Ma has no archival source whatsoever.
      </p>

      <h3>The scriptures people cite — what they actually say</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Nasadiya Sukta (Rig Veda 10.129)</strong> — the "Creation Hymn": <em>"Then was not non-existent nor
        existent: there was no realm of air, no sky beyond it... That One Thing, breathless, breathed by its own
        nature: apart from it was nothing whatsoever... Who verily knows and who can here declare it, whence it was
        born and whence comes this creation? ... he verily knows it, or perhaps he knows not."</em> Popular link: "neither
        existence nor non-existence" → the quantum vacuum, superposition, pre-Big-Bang states; "perhaps he does not
        know" → epistemic humility. The link is <strong>analogical only</strong>; no founder is documented citing it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Isha Upanishad, verses 4–5:</strong> <em>"Unmoving, the One is swifter than the mind... It moves. It
        moves not. It is far, and It is near. It is within all this, and It is outside of all this."</em> Popular link:
        "It moves and it moves not" → wave-particle duality and complementarity. Again analogical.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7 — "Tat Tvam Asi."</strong> Uddalaka Aruni to his son Svetaketu: <em>"That which
        is the finest essence — this whole world has that as its soul. That is reality. That is Atman. That art thou,
        Svetaketu."</em> The formula recurs nine times in the chapter, with analogies (salt dissolved in water; the seed
        containing the great fig tree). The Vedanta tradition reads it as the identity of atman and brahman — exactly the
        reading Schrödinger absorbed. <strong>This is the one passage a quantum founder himself explicitly and repeatedly
        invoked.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Mandukya Upanishad</strong> — twelve verses on Om and four states of consciousness: waking, dreaming, deep
        sleep, and "the fourth" (turiya) — <em>"unseen, incommunicable... peaceful, good, non-dual."</em> Popular link: states
        of consciousness → the measurement problem and the observer's role. Caveat: Schrödinger never cited the Mandukya
        specifically; his citations are to the Upanishads generally, the Chandogya formula, and Mundaka 2.2.11 ("I am in
        the east and in the west, I am below and above, I am this whole world").
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Bhagavad Gita, Chapter 11 (Vishvarupa-darshana)</strong> — Krishna grants Arjuna the vision of his cosmic,
        all-containing form; overwhelmed, Arjuna asks what this terrible radiance is for, and Krishna answers at 11.32
        (see above). Popular link: the all-containing form → quantum holism; <em>kāla</em> as all-devouring time →
        entropy. Oppenheimer's documented use is moral and existential — duty, detachment — not physical.
      </p>

      <p class="myth"><strong>Myth vs fact, in one table.</strong> "The 1925 pioneers found the solution in the
      Upanishads" — <strong>myth</strong> (Heisenberg's breakthroughs of 1925/1927 predate his 1929 Tagore meeting;
      no Upanishad supplied any equation). "Schrödinger derived wave mechanics from Vedanta" — <strong>unproven</strong>;
      deep philosophical engagement, verified; derivation, not evidenced. "Heisenberg was guided by Indian philosophy" —
      <strong>myth in the strong form</strong>; plausible in the weak form (it later helped him accept his own ideas).
      "Oppenheimer quoted the Gita at Trinity" — <strong>myth in the literal telling</strong> (attested only in 1965),
      <strong>verified in substance</strong> (Sanskrit from 1933, real engagement). "Bohr's yin-yang shows Hindu influence" —
      <strong>myth</strong> (Taoist symbol; zero documented Hindu engagement). "Access to the Vedas..." as Oppenheimer's
      line — <strong>misattribution</strong> (it's Schopenhauer's). The chronology is the simplest disinfectant.</p>

      <h3>How historians of science judge all this</h3>
      <p>
        The serious literature is thinner than the popular literature, and its authors are careful. Latten (2025, the
        only recent peer-reviewed study devoted to the topic) shows the engagement was specifically <strong>Shankara's
        Advaita</strong> as transmitted through Schopenhauer and Deussen — functioning as religious consolation <em>and</em>
        serious metaphysics with resonances in Schrödinger's philosophy of science, <strong>not as a source of physical
        theory</strong>. Moore's standard biography treats the Vedanta engagement at length but as worldview. Bitbol (1998)
        gives the strongest pro-influence scholarly formulation: the Vedantic-like view served as "a broad framework, and
        as a subliminal inspiration, in all parts of his work, including in theoretical physics" — still <em>subliminal</em>,
        not textual. Hijiya (2000) sets the scholarly standard for Oppenheimer: meticulous about what is documented, and
        reads the Gita as his <em>moral</em> vocabulary — duty, detachment — explicitly rejecting the grandiose
        "destroyer of worlds" reading. Wellerstein (2014) models the historian's corrective: dating the lore, quoting Frank
        Oppenheimer, insisting on the Arjuna-not-Krishna reading.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Bottom line:</strong> the documented history is (a) Schrödinger's deep, lifelong, textually specific Vedanta
        engagement as <em>philosophy</em>; (b) Heisenberg's single 1929 afternoon with Tagore, retrospectively meaningful
        to him; (c) Oppenheimer's Sanskrit/Gita study from 1933 and his genuine, morally inflected use of the Gita;
        (d) Bohr's Taoist — not Hindu — symbolism. Everything beyond that is myth, misattribution, or modern fabrication.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <section class="keypoints">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Schrödinger's engagement with Vedanta is the deepest documented case: Indian philosophy via Schopenhauer from 1918, the "doctrine of identity" around <em>Tat tvam asi</em> formulated in 1925, and lifelong writings — but no evidence it produced the mathematics of wave mechanics.</li>
      <li>Heisenberg met Tagore for one afternoon on 4 October 1929 — four years after matrix mechanics, two after the uncertainty principle. By his own account it later helped him accept ideas that had "seemed so crazy."</li>
      <li>Oppenheimer studied Sanskrit from 1933 to read the Gita in the original; "Now I am become Death" (Gita 11.32) is attested only in a 1965 interview, and scholars read him as Arjuna (duty, detachment) — not as Death.</li>
      <li>Bohr's yin-yang coat of arms (1947) is Taoist, symbolizing complementarity. No documented Bohr engagement with Hindu thought exists; the "Upanishads" quotes attributed to him are fabrications or misattributions of Schrödinger.</li>
      <li>Of the scriptures people cite — Nasadiya Sukta, Isha, Chandogya, Mandukya, Gita 11 — only Chandogya's <em>Tat Tvam Asi</em> was explicitly invoked by a founder (Schrödinger). The rest are modern analogies, not history.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

